# fn64 WM2000 route: the SAME 1P VS 2P card as entrance-to-match.schedule,
# with a REAL PLAYER 2.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
#
# `entrance-to-match.schedule` takes the default highlight on the player-count
# menu, which is "1P VS 2P" -- a local two-player card. It drives port 0 only,
# and the harness's `PifModel` populates port 0 and reports ports 1-3 as
# `CONT_ABSENT`. So that route plays a two-player match with one controller in
# the console: player 2's slot is a human slot with no human and no pad.
#
# This route is that route's port-0 column BYTE-FOR-BYTE, plus a port-1
# column. It is a separate file rather than an edit because
# `entrance-to-match.schedule` is committed evidence for the single-player
# path and is the pinned route for `render-benchmark.zsh`; changing it would
# invalidate every measurement quoted against sha a9e1b25e.
#
# HOW PORT 1 BECOMES PHYSICALLY PRESENT
#
# It is not an env var. The harness derives the hardware from the route:
# `ControllerInputSchedule::driven_ports()` reports which ports the file
# actually commands, and `attach_controllers_for_driven_ports` populates
# exactly those. A schedule therefore cannot declare presses for a port the
# harness left empty -- the two cannot disagree. Ports with only neutral
# phases do not count as driven, so padding a route with idle filler never
# silently changes what the guest probes.
#
# Format (crates/fn64-boot-harness/src/controller_input_schedule.rs):
#   port first_read end_read buttons_hex stick_x stick_y
# The clock is that PORT'S OWN read ordinal. Port 1's ordinals are counted
# independently of port 0's and only start advancing once the guest actually
# reads port 1 -- which it only does once the port answers a query as present.
#
# OSContPad.button bits: A=0x8000 B=0x4000 Z=0x2000 START=0x1000
#                        dU=0x0800 dD=0x0400 dL=0x0200 dR=0x0100
fn64.controller-input-schedule.v1

# =====================================================================
# PORT 0 -- identical to entrance-to-match.schedule. Do not diverge: the
# menu path is the evidenced one and any change here re-opens questions
# that route already answered off dumped frames.
# =====================================================================

# --- Boot splash sequence (THQ / JAKKS / AKI). Idle; the logos are timed.
0    0   90 0x0000 0 0

# --- Title screen: START to enter the main menu.
0   90  100 0x1000 0 0
0  100  130 0x0000 0 0

# --- Main menu: A confirms Exhibition (the default highlighted entry).
0  130  140 0x8000 0 0
0  140  180 0x0000 0 0

# --- Match-type menu: A confirms Single Match.
0  180  190 0x8000 0 0
0  190  230 0x0000 0 0

# --- Player-count menu: A takes the default highlight, 1P VS 2P.
0  230  240 0x8000 0 0
0  240  280 0x0000 0 0
0  280  290 0x8000 0 0
0  290  330 0x0000 0 0

# --- Rules / options page: A accepts the defaults.
0  330  340 0x8000 0 0
0  340  380 0x0000 0 0

# --- Match-setup page. A opens the Superstars roster.
0  380  390 0x8000 0 0
0  390  430 0x0000 0 0
0  430  440 0x1000 0 0
0  440  470 0x0000 0 0
0  470  480 0x8000 0 0
0  480  510 0x0000 0 0
0  510  520 0x1000 0 0
0  520  550 0x0000 0 0

# --- Superstars roster: A on the default highlight confirms a wrestler.
0  550  560 0x8000 0 0
0  560  600 0x0000 0 0
0  600  610 0x8000 0 0
0  610  700 0x0000 0 0

# --- Decision page: A commits it and the match loads.
0  700  710 0x8000 0 0
0  710  900 0x0000 0 0

# --- Versus presentation: a looping cinematic. START skips it.
0  900  910 0x1000 0 0
0  910 1000 0x0000 0 0
0 1000 1010 0x8000 0 0
0 1010 1200 0x0000 0 0
0 1200 1210 0x1000 0 0
0 1210 1900 0x0000 0 0

# --- IN-MATCH GAMEPLAY, player 1.
0 1900 1980 0x0000 80 0
0 1980 2060 0x0000 -80 0
0 2060 2070 0x8000 0 0
0 2070 2200 0x0000 0 0
0 2200 2280 0x0000 0 80
0 2280 3000 0x0000 0 0

# THE EVIDENCE THIS ROUTE PRODUCED, 2026-08-07, 1.5M steps, headless
#
#   entrance-to-match.schedule:  port0=2390  port1=0     controller_ops=2390
#   this route:                  port0=2957  port1=2957  controller_ops=5914
#
# Port 1 read ZERO times across a full match on the committed route -- not
# CPU-substituted, simply never polled, because `0x01` (read buttons) on an
# `Absent` port takes the `mark_no_response` arm in `fn64-abi/src/si/mod.rs`
# and records no read at all. With the port present the guest polls it on
# exactly the port-0 cadence. `render_error=None` on both.
#
# And it is visible, not merely counted. Two dumped frames at the SAME match
# clock (01:14), same arena, same dump index, differing only in whether port
# 1 was driven in-match:
#   reference/wm2000-frames/two-player-p2-idle-control.png -- port 1 neutral:
#     the wrestlers stand apart, player 1's ATTITUDE meter partial.
#   reference/wm2000-frames/two-player-p2-grapple.png -- port 1 driven:
#     the two have CLOSED AND LOCKED INTO A GRAPPLE at ring center, camera
#     repositioned, both ATTITUDE meters full.
# Player 2 moved toward player 1 and engaged. That is the same standard of
# evidence the in-match work used (an advancing clock and diverging meters),
# applied to the second pad.
#
# A control worth keeping: through the MENUS, where port 1 is held neutral,
# this route's heartbeat at step 400,000 is
# `gfx_submits=1758 audio_submits=1899 port0_reads=1014` -- identical to the
# single-player route's, with sim_time differing by 1,587 cycles out of 3.24
# billion (the extra SI DMA). Adding the second controller does not perturb
# the established path; it only adds a player.
#
# =====================================================================
# PORT 1 -- the second player.
#
# Port 1's read ordinal is its own clock, counted independently of port 0's.
#
# MEASURED, not assumed: once port 1 answers a query as present, WM2000
# polls it on the same cadence as port 0 from the very first frame. In the
# run that produced this file port 1 reached read 400 at step 184,225 while
# port 0 was near read 400 at step ~180,000 -- the two ordinals track each
# other roughly 1:1. So the in-match window is at the SAME ordinal here as
# it is on port 0, around read 1900, and the ranges below say so.
#
# (The first draft of this file put port 1's phases at reads 0-800, on the
# assumption that a second pad might be polled rarely or only once a match
# begins. The counter said otherwise -- those phases all fired during the
# menus, before the match existed. This is the corrected version.)
#
# Sticks, not buttons, in the menus: WM2000's menus advance on button
# EDGES, so a port-1 button press could race the port-0 menu path and
# change which screens the route visits, making this a different route
# rather than the same route with a second player. Port 1 is therefore
# held fully neutral until the match is live.
# =====================================================================

# Everything before read 1900: neutral is implicit (any read outside a
# phase returns the idle pad), so nothing is written. The port is still
# PRESENT throughout -- presence comes from being driven anywhere in the
# file, not from having a phase at read 0.

# --- IN-MATCH, player 2. Hard left, then hard right, then A -- the same
#     shape of input that proved player 1 controllable, on the other pad.
#     Port 1 moves RIGHT while port 0 moves LEFT over reads 1900-1980, so
#     the two wrestlers separate rather than tracking together: a frame in
#     which both moved the same way would not distinguish "player 2
#     responds" from "the camera panned".
1 1900 1980 0x0000 80 0
1 1980 2060 0x0000 -80 0
1 2060 2070 0x8000 0 0
1 2070 2200 0x0000 0 0
1 2200 2280 0x0000 0 -80
1 2280 3000 0x0000 0 0
